From de5b2ffdca938bdc4656057b6799b7ed22f8581b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 06:06:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] swresample/ppc: add AltiVec rematrix mixing kernels

New libswresample/ppc/rematrix.c with swri_rematrix_init_ppc (declared in
swresample_internal.h, dispatched from swri_rematrix_init under ARCH_PPC
exactly where the x86 init hooks in). HAVE_ALTIVEC && HAVE_BIGENDIAN gated,
PPC_ALTIVEC runtime check, registered on every AltiVec CPU (no per-CPU
gate: no bench data yet).

Kernels:
- mix_1_1 / mix_2_1 float (FLTP internal format): vec_madd on the
  native_simd_matrix float table, a plain copy of native_matrix like x86.
- mix_1_1 / mix_2_1 int16 (S16P): the same {int16 coef, int16 shift} pair
  table x86 builds (per-row pre-scaling so the coefficient fits an int16,
  bit-exact for every coefficient that is a multiple of the row's 2^sh --
  i.e. always when |coef| <= 1.0, the sum-normalised default matrices).
  Multiply-accumulate is the modular vec_msum on interleaved {in, 1} x
  {coef, round} / {in1, in2} x {c1, c2} halfword pairs, then vec_sra and
  the saturating vec_packs, which makes one kernel serve both the plain and
  the _clip_s16 C variants bit-exactly (the plain variant only ever sees
  results that fit int16). native_simd_one is set like x86 so the dither
  path's mix_2_1_simd(..., &s->native_simd_one, 0, 0, len) call works.
- mix6to2 / mix8to2 float: AltiVec versions of rematrix_template.c's
  mix_any_f special cases, installed over the C pointer when the generic
  init selected one (only the input channel count is left to tell 6 from
  8). These are the path every DEFAULT 5.1 -> stereo and 7.1 -> stereo
  downmix takes; mix_1_1/mix_2_1 are never called for it, so without them
  the common downmix would have stayed scalar. Same coefficient indexing
  and association as the C template, scalar tail for len % 4.

Alignment: swri_rematrix hands these the planes of whatever AudioData it
mixes, frequently the user's own buffers offset by arbitrary sample counts
(postin/preout alias the user input/output whenever no format conversion is
needed; the dither call offsets the noise plane by noise_pos), so every
pointer is only sample-aligned. Loads splice each 16-byte window from the
two bracketing aligned quadwords with one vec_lvsl permute per pointer (the
window stride is 16 bytes, so the residue is constant); stores use the
streaming vec_lvsr idiom from yuv2yuv_altivec.c's store16: middle quadwords
are plain vec_st of vec_perm(v[k-1], v[k], lvsr), and only the partial
first/last quadwords are written element-wise with vec_ste, so nothing
outside [out, out+len) is touched (no read-modify-write of neighbouring
planes). mix_1_1/mix_2_1 rely on swri_rematrix passing len & ~15 and
finishing the tail in C, as the x86 kernels do.

Validated under the qemu -cpu 7400 harness: standalone sweep (every 16-byte
residue of every in/out pointer, lengths 16..256 for the mixers and 1..70
for mixNto2, guard bytes around every output, int16 reference in int64 to
avoid the C template's own int32 overflow at >2x total gain) 0 mismatches;
checkasm swr_rematrix (new, previous commit) real ALTIVEC dispatch for all
float/int16 mix_1_1/mix_2_1 (plain and clip) and fltp mix6to2/mix8to2,
mutation-checked; full suite green. Real-G4/G5 --bench pending.

Left on C: S32P (int64 intermediates) and DBLP (no double vector before
VSX) mixers, and the s16 mix6to2/mix8to2 special cases (per-sample
products of 6-8 int16 x int coefficients need 32x32 widening MACs that
AltiVec lacks; the int16 pair-msum trick only covers two inputs per lane).

